---
import AtlasFigure from "../../components/AtlasFigure.astro";
import SiteShell from "../../components/SiteShell.astro";
---

<SiteShell
  title="Labs"
  description="Reproducible AIDD exercises with frozen data and evaluation contracts."
>
  <section aria-labelledby="labs-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">Reproducible exercises</p>
    <h1 id="labs-title" class="page-heading">Labs</h1>
    <p class="page-intro">
      Each lab names its input, output, version, and limitations. Predictive labs
      additionally freeze the split, metric, and baseline. This index does not
      report unrun or unmatched benchmark results.
    </p>
    <p class="section-intro">
      Look at the order of the sequence: the protocol is written and frozen before
      the held-out result is seen, the test set is opened once, and a bug found
      after that opening invalidates the protocol rather than producing a result.
    </p>
    <AtlasFigure id="frozen-lifecycle" />
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="notebook-route-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Start here · four notebook exercises</p>
      <h2 id="notebook-route-title">From representations to properties and cited evidence</h2>
      <p>Inspect seven molecules, learn fitting on invented data, then work through
        measured logD and inspect saved PaperQA answers against their sources.
        The default PaperQA lesson is offline; fresh inference is separately opt-in.
        The guide explains Colab upload, cell order, saving results
        and the separate role of completed evidence notebooks.</p>
      <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/notebook-guide/">Follow the notebook practice route</a>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="student-lab-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Editable student exercise · synthetic data</p>
      <h2 id="student-lab-title">Fit, select and explain a baseline</h2>
      <p>Open a self-contained notebook on ten invented entities. Inspect
        training, validation and errors without touching the frozen real-data labs.
        No dataset download, additional modelling package or GPU is needed. The
        standard-library command-line version remains available.</p>
      <a class="button-secondary" href="/learn/machine-learning/student-modelling-sandbox/">Open the student sandbox</a>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="lipophilicity-notebook-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Editable notebook-first exercise · fresh local results</p>
      <h2 id="lipophilicity-notebook-title">Lipophilicity: split, fit, inspect</h2>
      <p>
        Work through a self-contained notebook on public measured logD data: inspect
        molecules, keep scaffold groups intact, select simple baselines on validation
        data, and interpret plots and errors. Your run creates a fresh exercise result;
        it does not replace any frozen lab evaluation.
      </p>
      <p><a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/lipophilicity-notebook/">Open the notebook guide</a></p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="representation-lab-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Executable representation lab</p>
      <h2 id="representation-lab-title">Label-free molecule representation inspector</h2>
      <p>
        Compare canonical and non-isomeric SMILES, identifiers, descriptors,
        Morgan fingerprints, scaffolds, stereochemistry, and disconnected salts
        without loading labels or fitting a model.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/molecule-representation-inspector/"
          >Run the representation inspector</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="chembl-egfr-lab-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Completed frozen comparison</p>
      <h2 id="chembl-egfr-lab-title">ChEMBL 37 EGFR representation baselines</h2>
      <p>
        Compare 214 RDKit descriptors with a chirality-aware Morgan fingerprint
        under one parent-molecule contract and a scaffold-disjoint split.
        Primary and assay-replication evaluations completed; the Morgan transfer
        failure is retained alongside development evidence.
      </p>
      <p class="section-intro">
        Look at the two evaluations side by side: the baseline that wins the primary
        partition loses the assay replication, which is why the failure is kept next
        to the result instead of behind it.
      </p>
      <AtlasFigure id="representation-flips" />
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/chembl-egfr-representation-baselines/"
          >Study the frozen comparison</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="chembl-contract-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Runnable offline inspection</p>
      <h2 id="chembl-contract-title">Read the frozen contract before the model</h2>
      <p>
        Print the screening unit, the endpoint definition and the admission
        arithmetic of that frozen lab with one command that reads only committed
        files, then recheck that the protocol, development and locked artifacts
        still agree. No download, no network and no training.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/chembl-contract-inspection/"
          >Inspect the contract</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="pool-budget-contract-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Runnable offline inspection</p>
      <h2 id="pool-budget-contract-title">What a seed is not</h2>
      <p>
        Recompute the budget arithmetic and the reported recovery AUC from the
        raw checkpoint series, read what each policy was allowed to see, and see
        why five fixed seeds are not an error bar. The result that went against
        the method is printed rather than inferred.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/pool-budget-contract-inspection/"
          >Inspect the budget contract</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="design-space-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Runnable offline enumeration</p>
      <h2 id="design-space-title">Size does not buy reach</h2>
      <p>
        Enumerate three candidate spaces exactly and test four molecules against
        them. Growing a space fourfold reached one molecule and left a target
        differing by a single ring atom exactly as unreachable, while two spaces
        of equal size share no member at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/design-space/">Enumerate the spaces</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="docking-protocol-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Runnable offline inspection</p>
      <h2 id="docking-protocol-title">Same units, different scales</h2>
      <p>
        Separate the seven components a docking score comes out of. Two protocols
        differing in one component report -8.2 and -10.7 on the same ligand, no
        pair in the set may be compared, and a protocol without validation still
        produces a full column of numbers.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/docking-protocol/">Check comparability</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="egfr-structure-evidence-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Runnable offline inspection of deposited structures</p>
      <h2 id="egfr-structure-evidence-title">Three structures, read as evidence</h2>
      <p>
        Read three EGFR crystal structures from the PDB. The hinge methionine is
        residue 769 in two files and 793 in the third, a lapatinib pose clashes
        with 6 atoms in one receptor state and none in another, and two
        constructed poses pass a 2 A RMSD convention while one loses the hinge
        contact and the other clashes.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/egfr-structure-evidence/">Read the structures</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="safety-evidence-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Runnable offline inspection</p>
      <h2 id="safety-evidence-title">An alert is not a finding</h2>
      <p>
        Separate structural alerts from assay outcomes, organ liabilities, dose
        context and organism-level evidence. Seven of twelve compounds alert,
        five of those were never measured at all, and one of twelve carries any
        organism-level evidence.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/safety-evidence/"
          >Separate the levels</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="binder-definition-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Runnable offline demonstration</p>
      <h2 id="binder-definition-title">One set of molecules, several datasets</h2>
      <p>
        Hold the molecules fixed and move the definitions. The positive count
        more than doubles across a grid of cutoffs, four of twelve compounds are
        filed by noise, two thirds of the enlarged negative class was never
        measured, and six positives sit on three scaffolds.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/binder-definition/"
          >Move the definitions</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="sar-attribution-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Runnable offline demonstration</p>
      <h2 id="sar-attribution-title">The explanation pointed at the wrong atoms</h2>
      <p>
        Fit a model to data whose generating rule is known, explain it, and watch
        the credit land on a substructure that causes nothing. The confounder
        outscores the true cause on seven of eight actives, and the matched pair
        that breaks the correlation is ranked backwards.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/sar-attribution/"
          >Check the attribution</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="retro-disconnection-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Runnable offline comparison</p>
      <h2 id="retro-disconnection-title">The same score, three different causes</h2>
      <p>
        Compare rule, retrieval and template disconnections on one constructed
        route set. All three are perfect inside the precedent set and separate
        completely outside it, for three different reasons, and no recovery
        percentage says whether a route can be run.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/retro-disconnection/"
          >Compare the families</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="screening-transport-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Runnable offline inspection</p>
      <h2 id="screening-transport-title">Repeating is not transporting</h2>
      <p>
        Read a completed two-assay screening replay in units of recovered
        targets. The two contexts measure in steps 4.92 times apart, the whole
        baseline spread is under two targets in both, and the policy that
        recovered nothing lost by an eighth of a single target.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/screening-transport/"
          >Check the transport</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="property-decision-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Runnable offline inspection</p>
      <h2 id="property-decision-title">The band where you cannot decide</h2>
      <p>
        Apply a frozen threshold rule to calibrated FreeSolv intervals and read
        the abstention band a point estimate would have hidden. Only one of four
        combinations reaches the coverage it was built for, it is the one with
        the widest band, and it abstains the most.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/property-decision-threshold/"
          >Apply the frozen threshold</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="generation-gate-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Runnable offline inspection</p>
      <h2 id="generation-gate-title">Freeze the objective, then count the denominators</h2>
      <p>
        Declare a design objective that can actually be applied, then screen
        twenty candidates through six gates whose denominators chain. See why the
        headline valid figure and the survivor count differ, why memorization is
        the novelty gate against a narrower set, and what a synthesis proxy never
        establishes. No generative model is run.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/generation-gate/"
          >Apply the frozen objective</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="counterevidence-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Runnable offline inspection</p>
      <h2 id="counterevidence-title">What a clean summary would have deleted</h2>
      <p>
        Inventory six kinds of counterevidence preserved across the completed lab
        artifacts, then recompute the transfer failure that reporting one
        comparison alone would have hidden: the model with the lowest error on
        one evaluation is several times the baseline error on the other.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/counterevidence-dossier/"
          >Inspect the dossier</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="cofolding-claims-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Runnable offline inspection</p>
      <h2 id="cofolding-claims-title">Five claims that arrive in one file</h2>
      <p>
        Separate monomer prediction, complex prediction, co-folding, pose
        generation and experimental binding evidence across twenty registered
        systems, and see where a modality label, a dual-claim model and a missing
        measurement record invite you to quote one as evidence for another.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/cofolding-claim-separation/"
          >Separate the claims</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="rmd17-contract-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Runnable offline inspection</p>
      <h2 id="rmd17-contract-title">What the error is an error over</h2>
      <p>
        Print the observable, the prohibited feature arrays, the frame sampling
        with its measured gaps, and every error beside the range it is measured
        over, then recheck fourteen relationships across the frozen rMD17
        artifacts. No download, no network and no fitting.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/rmd17-contract-inspection/"
          >Inspect the metric contract</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="rmd17-lab-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Completed system-state baseline</p>
      <h2 id="rmd17-lab-title">rMD17 system-state energy baselines</h2>
      <p>
        Trace a frozen original-index split from molecular system state through
        per-system pair-distance histograms and independently fit Ridge models.
        Benzene and uracil evaluations completed; each model uses its own system's
        training frames, with the missing per-frame Ridge prediction record disclosed.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a
          class="button-secondary"
          href="/labs/rmd17-system-state-energy-baselines/"
          >Study the rMD17 evidence</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="pool-budget-lab-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Synthetic simulator and completed assay replay</p>
      <h2 id="pool-budget-lab-title">Pool-budget campaign simulator</h2>
      <p>Compare label-blind MaxMin and seeded random selection at equal integer
        budgets. The authored synthetic example is separate from the completed
        real ChEMBL replay, including its negative results.</p>
      <p><a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/pool-budget-campaign/">Run the pool-budget simulator</a></p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="rmd17-dynamics-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Fresh reproduction over an existing trajectory</p>
      <h2 id="rmd17-dynamics-title">A trajectory observable, and how much of it you need</h2>
      <p>
        Compute the mass-weighted radius of gyration from the same committed
        rMD17 trajectories the energy lab already uses, for a different
        question. A naive standard error understates ethanol's uncertainty by
        a factor of 3.30 and malonaldehyde's by 6.26; at 10,000 frames
        malonaldehyde's cumulative mean still sits four to nine block-standard-
        errors from its converged value, despite matching it to two decimal
        places.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/rmd17-dynamics-observable/"
          >Inspect the observable</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="pool-budget-uncertainty-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Real ensemble-variance acquisition policy</p>
      <h2 id="pool-budget-uncertainty-title">An acquisition policy that reads its own model's disagreement</h2>
      <p>
        Add a seventh policy to the completed pool-budget campaign: acquire by
        cross-fold Ridge prediction variance instead of chemical distance or
        chance. It leads MaxMin and all five fixed random seeds on both
        assays, 0.1133 against MaxMin's 0.0508 on the primary assay and 0.1635
        against 0.0000 on the separate one, from one deterministic run under
        the same real partition and budget.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/pool-budget-uncertainty-acquisition/"
          >Read the acquisition result</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="cyp-assay-system-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Fresh computation over a live ChEMBL snapshot</p>
      <h2 id="cyp-assay-system-title">Does one CYP inhibition number predict another?</h2>
      <p>
        Read real ChEMBL 37 IC50 records across two whole cytochrome P450
        targets instead of two named assays: 5,251 admitted CYP3A4 parents and
        2,781 CYP2D6, with 1,195 overlapping. Potency against one enzyme
        predicts potency against the other only weakly, Spearman rho about
        0.30, and quinidine alone carries 32 CYP2D6 records disagreeing by
        more than a 10,000-fold range beside a single CYP3A4 value.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/cyp-assay-system/"
          >Compare the two targets</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="decision-unit-scope-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Fresh computation over a live ChEMBL snapshot</p>
      <h2 id="decision-unit-scope-title">One reported value is one row in a context</h2>
      <p>
        Take fifteen declared compounds through ChEMBL 37 and count what a
        reported value actually rests on. Thirteen thousand three hundred and
        five activity rows reduce to one thousand and four admitted
        measurements once the endpoint family, the unit, the standard flag, the
        duplicate check, the data-validity comment and the relation are
        applied, and the fifteen seeds resolve to ten distinct parents. The
        lab then reports the assay, document, target, organism, assay type,
        confidence score and relation that travel with each surviving
        measurement, because that context is what makes a comparison mean
        something.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/decision-unit-scope/"
          >Count the measurements</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="assay-endpoint-controls-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Fresh computation over six archived public assays</p>
      <h2 id="assay-endpoint-controls-title">What an assay actually contains</h2>
      <p>
        Read six archived PubChem BioAssay dose-response assays and report
        what each one holds. One thousand five hundred and twenty-nine
        admitted endpoint values spread over five endpoint labels, and of the
        one thousand two hundred and fifty-seven that carry a stated qualifier,
        eight hundred and thirty-two are equalities while four hundred and six
        are bounds that state a side and not a magnitude. The confidence score
        sits at zero for a third of the values. Each assay also reports its
        declared replicate structure, its control regime, its reference wells
        and its own normalisation equation, because an assay licenses a
        conclusion only as far as its control record supports it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/assay-endpoint-controls/"
          >Read the assays</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="rosetta-cross-modality-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Fresh computation over a public perturbation archive</p>
      <h2 id="rosetta-cross-modality-title">Two readouts of one perturbation</h2>
      <p>
        Five hundred and twenty-eight perturbations measured in both a
        transcriptomic and an imaging readout, across sixteen plates each,
        look strongly correlated: feature level r 0.83 and perturbation level
        r 0.82. Blocking the comparison on the batch drops that to 0.63 and
        0.69, and a size-matched random control reaches 0.79 to 0.81. Most of
        the apparent agreement is structure rather than biology, and the lab
        measures how much of it survives.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/rosetta-cross-modality/"
          >Compare the two readouts</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="az-clearance-system-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Fresh computation over a live ChEMBL snapshot</p>
      <h2 id="az-clearance-system-title">The same clearance, three ways of measuring it</h2>
      <p>
        Compare intrinsic clearance across human liver microsomes, rat
        hepatocytes and human hepatocytes from one real AstraZeneca ChEMBL
        deposition: 743, 592 and 288 admitted parents, correlating at Spearman
        rho 0.39 to 0.49 pairwise. Microsome values are reported per gram of
        protein and hepatocyte values per million cells, so only the rank
        correlations, not a raw fold-change, compare validly across a matrix
        change.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/az-clearance-system/"
          >Compare the three assays</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="l1000-perturbation-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Fresh computation over a real, cached LINCS L1000 snapshot</p>
      <h2 id="l1000-perturbation-title">The correlation was real, at ten times too high</h2>
      <p>
        Compare compound-perturbation gene-expression signatures between MCF7
        and PC3 across 1,721 compounds shared in one real LINCS L1000 series.
        On raw expression, matched and mismatched compounds correlate almost
        identically, rho 0.79 against 0.70; subtracting each profile's own
        plate vehicle-control baseline first reveals the real signal, 0.175
        against a null of 0.034 (p ≈ 8 × 10⁻⁸²).
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/l1000-perturbation-context/"
          >Read the corrected signature</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="freesolv-lab-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Completed frozen protocol</p>
      <h2 id="freesolv-lab-title">FreeSolv v0.52 honest baselines</h2>
      <p>
        One final frozen test evaluation is recorded. The scaffold-primary Ridge
        result is MAE 1.1280482645936036 and RMSE 1.7597471244251413 kcal/mol;
        this is an honest baseline, not a SOTA claim.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/freesolv-honest-baselines/"
          >Read the completed lab</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="objective-ladder-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Fresh computation over public records</p>
      <h2 id="objective-ladder-title">An objective ladder for public protein records</h2>
      <p>
        Read sequence, structure, affinity and annotation records for 6 examined
        entities across 6 objectives. The laboratory checks which source can
        support each cell, with a record in 31 of 36 cells, and keeps the
        source-specific reasons for the empty cells visible.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/objective-ladder/"
          >Read the objective ladder</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="asset-openness-audit-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Fresh computation over the committed catalogue</p>
      <h2 id="asset-openness-audit-title">Seven openness fields, audited apart</h2>
      <p>
        Read the resource atlas and check that every record keeps inference
        code, training code, weights, architecture, data recipe, training data
        and evaluation code as separate fields, each with its own evidence, and
        that every licence says what its grant does not reach. Over the
        examined records 52 claims are available, 26 do not apply, 88 are
        unverified and 2 are recorded as not available, and all 191 records
        keep the seven fields apart.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/labs/asset-openness-audit/"
          >Audit the openness fields</a
        >
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="split-leakage-audit-title">
    <article class="detail-panel">
      <p class="meta-label">Fresh computation over a live ChEMBL snapshot</p>
      <h2 id="split-leakage-audit-title">Which identities cross a row split</h2>
      <p>
        Take the 486 independent measurements the decision-unit laboratory
        admits from fifteen ChEMBL seeds, split them at random, and count the
        test measurements that share a parent, a scaffold, an assay, a target
        or a standard form with the training set. All 98 test measurements
        share a parent with the training set, and four routes, including
        temporal and patient leakage, cannot be measured on this table at all.
      </p>
      <p>
